<!-- Section 6: Key Takeaways -->
<section id="key-takeaways">
  <h2>6. Key Takeaways &amp; Strategic Implications</h2>

  <ol class="takeaway-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Amsterdam is the most protected, least commercialized market.</strong>
      With a median availability of 0 days, only 6.8% professional host share, and the highest price point (median $222), Amsterdam exemplifies a market shaped by aggressive regulation. The result: constrained supply, premium pricing for the listings that remain legal, and almost no large-scale commercial operators. Investors should treat Amsterdam as a high-barrier, high-margin market where regulatory risk is front-loaded (compliance costs) rather than backend (ban risk).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Bangkok and Barcelona are deeply commercialized — and exposed.</strong>
      With professional host shares of 60.4% and 58.3% respectively, both markets are dominated by multi-listing operators. In Bangkok, one host controls 816 listings. This concentration creates single points of supply risk: if a major operator exits, availability shifts materially. For regulators, these cities represent the strongest case for supply-side interventions. For investors, they signal mature, competitive markets where differentiation (not just inventory) is the path to returns.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rome outperforms on guest demand across every metric.</strong>
      Rome leads in average reviews/month (1.77), total reviews (60.0 avg), and reviews in the last twelve months (14.4). Combined with a moderate professional host share (30.9%) and strong availability (218.6 days), Rome presents as a balanced, high-demand market with room for both individual and professional hosts. It is the most attractive market for new entrants based on demand fundamentals alone.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>London is the largest market but a "volume game" with thin margins.</strong>
      London has the most listings (16,674) but the second-lowest median price ($135) and below-average reviews/month (0.99). With 32.2% professional host share, it's moderately commercialized. The sheer volume of supply — 3.3x more listings than Rome — creates intense competition. London rewards scale: investors need portfolio size to extract meaningful returns from a low-margin, high-competition environment.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Paris is the data blind spot — largest European market, zero price visibility.</strong>
      With 13,927 listings (27.9% of the dataset) but entirely missing price data, Paris cannot be evaluated on pricing metrics. What IS visible: 88.2% entire-home share (highest), 21.4% professional host share (second-lowest), and moderate demand metrics (1.01 reviews/month). The high entire-home share with low professional concentration suggests many individual owners renting entire properties — a pattern consistent with Paris's strong second-home and pied-à-terre culture.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Availability tells the regulatory story better than any policy document.</strong>
      The gradient from Amsterdam (92.5 days avg) to Bangkok (252.5 days) maps cleanly onto regulatory restrictiveness. The share of listings with 30+ night minimums (Amsterdam 8.4%, Barcelona 3.4%, London 1.7%) is a reliable regulatory heatmap. Investors should track availability and minimum-night distributions as leading indicators of regulatory shifts — tightening usually shows up in these metrics before it appears in legislation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Bangkok's price data is a red flag for cross-market comparability.</strong>
      Bangkok's median price of $1,400/night is ~10x the European average and almost certainly reflects local currency (THB) rather than normalized USD. At approximate exchange rates (~34 THB/USD), Bangkok's true median would be ~$41/night — the cheapest market by far. Without currency normalization, Bangkok cannot be meaningfully compared to other cities on price. Any investment analysis must verify currency conversions independently.
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

