<!-- Section 6: Key Takeaways -->
<section id="key-takeaways">
  <h2>6. Key Takeaways &amp; Strategic Implications</h2>

  <ol class="takeaway-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Amsterdam is the most regulated market &mdash; high barriers, premium pricing.</strong>
      Median availability of just 18 days/year, only 6.8% professional host share, and the highest validated median price ($222) define Amsterdam as a market shaped by aggressive short-term rental regulation. The 30-night annual cap for entire homes has effectively excluded commercial operators (only 58 professional hosts out of 1,676). For investors, Amsterdam offers high margins but requires careful regulatory compliance; for policymakers, it is the clearest case study of supply-side regulation achieving its intended structural effects.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Bangkok and Barcelona are deeply commercialized &mdash; and exposed to policy risk.</strong>
      With professional host shares of 60.4% and 58.3%, both markets are dominated by multi-listing operators. Barcelona's 514-listing maximum and Bangkok's 243-listing maximum indicate industrial-scale operators. Barcelona adds a second dimension of risk: 40.1% of listings require 30+ night minimum stays, revealing an active regulatory regime. Any tightening of short-term rental laws in either city would disproportionately affect a small number of large operators, creating concentrated downside risk.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rome is the most attractive market for new entrants, based on demand fundamentals.</strong>
      Rome leads every demand metric: 1.77 reviews/month, 60.0 average total reviews, 14.4 average LTM reviews. Combined with moderate professional host share (30.9%), strong availability (218.6 days), and competitive pricing ($131 median), Rome presents a balanced market with demand depth, room for individual operators, and relatively low regulatory friction. The 85.4% review rate suggests genuine booking activity rather than speculative listings.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>London is a scale-play: massive volume, thin margins, intense competition.</strong>
      At 16,674 listings, London is 3.3x larger than Rome. But it has the second-lowest median price ($135), below-average reviews/month (0.99), and moderate commercialization (32.2% professional). London rewards scale: investors need portfolio breadth to extract meaningful returns from a low-margin, high-competition environment. Individual hosts face significant discoverability challenges in the largest inventory pool.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Paris is a data blind spot for pricing, but its supply structure tells a clear story.</strong>
      With 13,927 listings (27.9% of dataset) but zero valid prices, Paris cannot be assessed on pricing or value. What is visible: 88.2% entire-home share (highest), 21.4% professional host share (second lowest), and a single operator with 816 listings dominating a field of mostly individual hosts. The combination of high entire-home share with low professional concentration suggests many individual owners renting entire properties &mdash; a pattern consistent with Paris's strong second-home owner culture. But without price data, any investment thesis for Paris is incomplete.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The 30+ night minimum is the single best regulatory fingerprint in this dataset.</strong>
      Barcelona (40.1%), Paris (20.4%), Sydney (17.1%), and Bangkok (13.8%) all show elevated 30+ night minimum rates. Barcelona and Paris align with known regulatory constraints (Catalonia's licensing system, France's primary residence limits). Sydney's 17.1% rate may reflect New South Wales's 180-day cap on non-hosted short-term rentals. Investors should track minimum-night distributions as a leading indicator of regulatory tightening &mdash; these metrics often shift before policy changes are formalized.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Private rooms consistently outperform entire homes on guest engagement.</strong>
      Across all 7 cities, private rooms generate higher reviews per month than entire homes. In Amsterdam, private rooms average 1.74 vs. 0.87 for entire homes &mdash; a 2x gap. In Paris, the gap is 1.87 vs. 0.90. This suggests that private rooms, while lower-priced, achieve higher booking frequency &mdash; a potentially attractive segment for hosts prioritizing occupancy over nightly rate. For investors, the private-room segment in high-cost cities (Amsterdam, Paris, London) may offer better risk-adjusted returns than entire homes.
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

