num">0.49</td><td class="num">27.3</td><td class="num">6.8</td><td class="num">78.3%</td><td class="num">281</td></tr>", "+        <tr><td>London</td><td class="num">0.99</td><td class="num">0.37</td><td class="num">22.2</td><td class="num">5.8</td><td class="num">75.5%</td><td class="num">273</td></tr>", "+        <tr><td>Bangkok</td><td class="num">0.96</td><td class="num">0.37</td><td class="num">19.6</td><td class="num">5.7</td><td class="num">64.4%</td><td class="num">282</td></tr>", "       </tbody>", "     </table>", "   </div>", " ", "   <div class="callout">", "-    <strong>Demand Leader:</strong> Rome's 1.77 reviews/month average (50%+ higher than London, Paris, and Bangkok) strongly suggests the highest guest booking frequency among the analyzed markets. This is consistent with Rome's position as a year-round destination with a compact, high-demand core. The gap between Rome's mean (1.77) and median (1.40) vs. Barcelona's mean (1.43) and median (0.52) reveals different distribution shapes — Rome has broad-based demand, while Barcelona's demand is more concentrated among top-performing listings.", "+    <strong>Demand Leader:</strong> Rome's 1.77 reviews/month average is 50%+ higher than London, Paris, and Bangkok. The gap between Rome's mean (1.77) and median (1.40) vs. Barcelona's mean (1.43) and median (0.52) reveals fundamentally different demand distributions: Rome has broad-based demand across most listings, while Barcelona's demand is concentrated among a subset of top performers. Bangkok's low engagement (0.96 rpm, only 64% with any reviews) and the high number of days since last review across all cities (232&ndash;300 days median) indicate a significant portion of listings may be inactive or rarely booked.", "   </div>", " ", "-  <div class="chart-row">", "-    <div class="chart-box">", "-      <h4>Review Recency: % of Listings Reviewed in Last 90 Days</h4>", "-      <canvas id="chart-recent-reviews"></canvas>", "-    </div>", "-    <div class="chart-box">", "-      <h4>Total Reviews vs. Reviews/Month — City Positioning</h4>", "-      <canvas id="chart-review-scatter"></canvas>", "-    </div>", "+  <div class="chart-row single">", "+    <div class="chart-box"><h4>Total Reviews vs. Reviews/Month &mdash; City Positioning Map</h4><canvas id="chart-review-scatter"></canvas></div>", "   </div>", " </section>", " ", " <!-- Section 5: Availability & Booking Constraints -->", " <section id="availability-minimumnights">", "   <h2>5. Availability &amp; Booking Constraints</h2>", "-  <p>Availability patterns and minimum-night requirements reveal both commercial intent and the imprint of local regulations. High availability signals listings operated primarily as short-term rentals, while low availability may indicate owner-occupied or part-time rentals. Elevated minimum-night requirements often correlate with regulations designed to protect long-term housing.</p>", "+  <p>Availability patterns and minimum-night requirements reveal the regulatory and commercial character of each market. High availability signals purpose-built short-term rental inventory; low availability often indicates regulatory constraints or owner-occupied part-time rentals. Elevated minimum-night thresholds frequently correlate with regulations that restrict short-term stays.</p>", " ", "   <div class="chart-row">", "-    <div class="chart-box">", "-      <h4>Average Annual Availability (Days/Year)</h4>", "-      <canvas id="chart-avail"></canvas>", "-    </div>", "-    <div class="chart-box">", "-      <h4>High Availability Share (% of listings available 200+ days/year)</h4>", "-      <canvas id="chart-high-avail"></canvas>", "-    </div>", "+    <div class="chart-box"><h4>Average Annual Availability (Days/Year)</h4><canvas id="chart-avail"></canvas></div>", "+    <div class="chart-box"><h4>High Availability Share (% of listings available 200+ days/year)</h4><canvas id="chart-high-avail"></canvas></div>", "   </div>", " ", "   <h3>Availability &amp; Booking Constraints Table</h3>", "   <div class="card">", "     <table class="data-table">", "-      <thead>", "-        <tr>", "-          <th>City</th>", "-          <th class="num">Avg Availability (days)</th>", "-          <th class="num">Median Availability</th>", "-          <th class="num">% Available 200+ Days</th>", "-          <th class="num">Median Min Nights</th>", "-          <th class="num">% 1-Night Min</th>", "-          <th class="num">% 30+ Night Min</th>", "-        </tr>", "-      </thead>", "+      <thead><tr><th>City</th><th class="num">Avg Avail. (days)</th><th class="num">Median Avail.</th><th class="num">% Avail. 200+ Days</th><th class="num">Median Min Nights</th><th class="num">% 1-Night Min</th><th class="num">% 30+ Night Min</th></tr></thead>", "       <tbody>", "-        <tr><td>Bangkok</td><td class="num rank">252.5</td><td class="num">285</td><td class="num">59.6%</td><td class="num"><span class="flag-badge low">2</span></td><td class="num">43.2%</td><td class="num">0.8%</td></tr>", "-        <tr><td>Rome</td><td class="num rank">218.6</td><td class="num">248</td><td class="num">51.5%</td><td class="num"><span class="flag-badge low">2</span></td><td class="num">40.7%</td><td class="num">1.3%</td></tr>", "-        <tr><td>Barcelona</td><td class="num rank">193.9</td><td class="num">205</td><td class="num">42.8%</td><td class="num"><span class="flag-badge med">3</span></td><td class="num">28.9%</td><td class="num">3.4%</td></tr>", "-        <tr><td>Sydney</td><td class="num rank">176.2</td><td class="num">161</td><td class="num">35.6%</td><td class="num"><span class="flag-badge low">2</span></td><td class="num">29.1%</td><td class="num">0.9%</td></tr>", "-        <tr><td>London</td><td class="num rank">146.0</td><td class="num">80</td><td class="num">27.3%</td><td class="num"><span class="flag-badge low">2</span></td><td class="num">31.3%</td><td class="num">1.7%</td></tr>", "-        <tr><td>Paris</td><td class="num rank">125.2</td><td class="num">10</td><td class="num">26.0%</td><td class="num"><span class="flag-badge med">3</span></td><td class="num">17.2%</td><td class="num">1.9%</td></tr>", "-        <tr><td>Amsterdam</td><td class="num rank">92.5</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">14.9%</td><td class="num"><span class="flag-badge med">3</span></td><td class="num">27.4%</td><td class="num">8.4%</td></tr>", "+        <tr><td>Bangkok</td><td class="num rank">252.5</td><td class="num">295</td><td class="num">70.1%</td><td class="num">2</td><td class="num">44.9%</td><td class="num"><span class="flag-badge high">13.8%</span></td></tr>", "+        <tr><td>Rome</td><td class="num">218.6</td><td class="num">253</td><td class="num">62.0%</td><td class="num">2</td><td class="num">37.0%</td><td class="num"><span class="flag-badge med">10.0%</span></td></tr>", "+        <tr><td>Barcelona</td><td class="num">193.9</td><td class="num">220</td><td class="num">53.2%</td><td class="num">3</td><td class="num">31.2%</td><td class="num"><span class="flag-badge danger" style="background:#fed7d7;color:#c53030;">40.1%</span></td></tr>", "+        <tr><td>Sydney</td><td class="num">176.2</td><td class="num">171</td><td class="num">44.0%</td><td class="num">2</td><td class="num">25.6%</td><td class="num"><span class="flag-badge high">17.1%</span></td></tr>", "+        <tr><td>London</td><td class="num">146.0</td><td class="num">102</td><td class="num">38.1%</td><td class="num">2</td><td class="num">33.9%</td><td class="num"><span class="flag-badge low">2.8%</span></td></tr>", "+        <tr><td>Paris</td><td class="num">125.2</td><td class="num">70</td><td class="num">33.4%</td><td class="num">3</td><td class="num">20.8%</td><td class="num"><span class="flag-badge high">20.4%</span></td></tr>", "+        <tr><td>Amsterdam</td><td class="num">92.5</td><td class="num">18</td><td class="num">22.2%</td><td class="num">3</td><td class="num">19.1%</td><td class="num"><span class="flag-badge low">1.2%</span></td></tr>", "       </tbody>", "     </table>", "   </div>", " ", "-  <div class="callout warning">", "-    <strong>Regulatory Footprint:</strong> Amsterdam's low 92.5-day average availability and <em>median of 0 days</em> directly reflect its strict 30-night annual cap on entire-home rentals. The 8.4% share of listings with 30+ night minimums — the highest across all cities — is a regulatory signature: hosts use minimum-night thresholds to target mid-term stays that fall outside short-term rental restrictions. Barcelona's 3.4% 30+ night share similarly points to regulatory pressure. Bangkok, by contrast, shows almost no regulatory friction, with 60% of listings available 200+ days/year.", "+  <div class="callout danger">", "+    <strong>Barcelona's 40.1% 30+ Night Minimum &mdash; A Regulatory Signature:</strong> Four out of ten Barcelona listings require a 30+ night minimum stay. This is not a market preference; it is the structural signature of Catalonia's short-term rental regulations (which impose licensing requirements and caps on short stays). Combined with Barcelona's 58.3% professional host share, this creates a bifurcated market: licensed short-term operators competing for tourist demand, and unlicensed or mid-term operators targeting the 30+ day segment. Paris (20.4% 30+ nights) shows a similar but less extreme pattern, consistent with France's primary residence rental caps. Bangkok's elevated 13.8% 30+ rate likely reflects a different dynamic (serviced apartments and long-stay tourism) rather than regulatory pressure.", "   </div>", " ", "   <div class="chart-row single">", "-    <div class="chart-box">", "-      <h4>Availability vs. Demand: Positioning Each Market</h4>", "-      <canvas id="chart-avail-demand"></canvas>", "-    </div>", "+    <div class="chart-box"><h4>Availability vs. Demand &mdash; Strategic Market Positioning</h4><canvas id="chart-avail-demand"></canvas></div>", "   </div>", " </section>", " "]}, {"oldStart": 617, "oldLines": 65, "newStart": 355, "newLines": 56, "lines": [" ", "   <ol class="takeaway-list">", "     <li>", "-      <strong>Amsterdam is the most protected, least commercialized market.</strong>", "-      With a median availability of 0 days, only 6.8% professional host share, and the highest price point (median $222), Amsterdam exemplifies a market shaped by aggressive regulation. The result: constrained supply, premium pricing for the listings that remain legal, and almost no large-scale commercial operators. Investors should treat Amsterdam as a high-barrier, high-margin market where regulatory risk is front-loaded (compliance costs) rather than backend (ban risk).", "+      <strong>Amsterdam is the most regulated market &mdash; high barriers, premium pricing.</strong>", "+      Median availability of just 18 days/year, only 6.8% professional host share, and the highest validated median price ($222) define Amsterdam as a market shaped by aggressive short-term rental regulation. The 30-night annual cap for entire homes has effectively excluded commercial operators (only 58 professional hosts out of 1,676). For investors, Amsterdam offers high margins but requires careful regulatory compliance; for policymakers, it is the clearest case study of supply-side regulation achieving its intended structural effects.", "     </li>", "     <li>", "-      <strong>Bangkok and Barcelona are deeply commercialized — and exposed.</strong>", "-      With professional host shares of 60.4% and 58.3% respectively, both markets are dominated by multi-listing operators. In Bangkok, one host controls 816 listings. This concentration creates single points of supply risk: if a major operator exits, availability shifts materially. For regulators, these cities represent the strongest case for supply-side interventions. For investors, they signal mature, competitive markets where differentiation (not just inventory) is the path to returns.", "+      <strong>Bangkok and Barcelona are deeply commercialized &mdash; and exposed to policy risk.</strong>", "+      With professional host shares of 60.4% and 58.3%, both markets are dominated by multi-listing operators. Barcelona's 514-listing maximum and Bangkok's 243-listing maximum indicate industrial-scale operators. Barcelona adds a second dimension of risk: 40.1% of listings require 30+ night minimum stays, revealing an active regulatory regime. Any tightening of short-term rental laws in either city would disproportionately affect a small number of large operators, creating concentrated downside risk.", "     </li>", "     <li>", "-      <strong>Rome outperforms on guest demand across every metric.</strong>", "-      Rome leads in average reviews/month (1.77), total reviews (60.0 avg), and reviews in the last twelve months (14.4). Combined with a moderate professional host share (30.9%) and strong availability (218.6 days), Rome presents as a balanced, high-demand market with room for both individual and professional hosts. It is the most attractive market for new entrants based on demand fundamentals alone.", "+      <strong>Rome is the most attractive market for new entrants, based on demand fundamentals.</strong>", "+      Rome leads every demand metric: 1.77 reviews/month, 60.0 average total reviews, 14.4 average LTM reviews. Combined with moderate professional host share (30.9%), strong availability (218.6 days), and competitive pricing ($131 median), Rome presents a balanced market with demand depth, room for individual operators, and relatively low regulatory friction. The 85.4% review rate suggests genuine booking activity rather than speculative listings.", "     </li>", "     <li>", "-      <strong>London is the largest market but a "volume game" with thin margins.</strong>", "-      London has the most listings (16,674) but the second-lowest median price ($135) and below-average reviews/month (0.99). With 32.2% professional host share, it's moderately commercialized. The sheer volume of supply — 3.3x more listings than Rome — creates intense competition. London rewards scale: investors need portfolio size to extract meaningful returns from a low-margin, high-competition environment.", "+      <strong>London is a scale-play: massive volume, thin margins, intense competition.</strong>", "+      At 16,674 listings, London is 3.3x larger than Rome. But it has the second-lowest median price ($135), below-average reviews/month (0.99), and moderate commercialization (32.2% professional). London rewards scale: investors need portfolio breadth to extract meaningful returns from a low-margin, high-competition environment. Individual hosts face significant discoverability challenges in the largest inventory pool.", "     </li>", "     <li>", "-      <strong>Paris is the data blind spot — largest European market, zero price visibility.</strong>", "-      With 13,927 listings (27.9% of the dataset) but entirely missing price data, Paris cannot be evaluated on pricing metrics. What IS visible: 88.2% entire-home share (highest), 21.4% professional host share (second-lowest), and moderate demand metrics (1.01 reviews/month). The high entire-home share with low professional concentration suggests many individual owners renting entire properties — a pattern consistent with Paris's strong second-home and pied-à-terre culture.", "+      <strong>Paris is a data blind spot for pricing, but its supply structure tells a clear story.</strong>", "+      With 13,927 listings (27.9% of dataset) but zero valid prices, Paris cannot be assessed on pricing or value. What is visible: 88.2% entire-home share (highest), 21.4% professional host share (second lowest), and a single operator with 816 listings dominating a field of mostly individual hosts. The combination of high entire-home share with low professional concentration suggests many individual owners renting entire properties &mdash; a pattern consistent with Paris's strong second-home owner culture. But without price data, any investment thesis for Paris is incomplete.", "     </li>", "     <li>", "-      <strong>Availability tells the regulatory story better than any policy document.</strong>", "-      The gradient from Amsterdam (92.5 days avg) to Bangkok (252.5 days) maps cleanly onto regulatory restrictiveness. The share of listings with 30+ night minimums (Amsterdam 8.4%, Barcelona 3.4%, London 1.7%) is a reliable regulatory heatmap. Investors should track availability and minimum-night distributions as leading indicators of regulatory shifts — tightening usually shows up in these metrics before it appears in legislation.", "+      <strong>The 30+ night minimum is the single best regulatory fingerprint in this dataset.</strong>", "+      Barcelona (40.1%), Paris (20.4%), Sydney (17.1%), and Bangkok (13.8%) all show elevated 30+ night minimum rates. Barcelona and Paris align with known regulatory constraints (Catalonia's licensing system, France's primary residence limits). Sydney's 17.1% rate may reflect New South Wales's 180-day cap on non-hosted short-term rentals. Investors should track minimum-night distributions as a leading indicator of regulatory tightening &mdash; these metrics often shift before policy changes are formalized.", "     </li>", "     <li>", "-      <strong>Bangkok's price data is a red flag for cross-market comparability.</strong>", "-      Bangkok's median price of $1,400/night is ~10x the European average and almost certainly reflects local currency (THB) rather than normalized USD. At approximate exchange rates (~34 THB/USD), Bangkok's true median would be ~$41/night — the cheapest market by far. Without currency normalization, Bangkok cannot be meaningfully compared to other cities on price. Any investment analysis must verify currency conversions independently.", "+      <strong>Private rooms consistently outperform entire homes on guest engagement.</strong>", "+      Across all 7 cities, private rooms generate higher reviews per month than entire homes. In Amsterdam, private rooms average 1.74 vs. 0.87 for entire homes &mdash; a 2x gap. In Paris, the gap is 1.87 vs. 0.90. This suggests that private rooms, while lower-priced, achieve higher booking frequency &mdash; a potentially attractive segment for hosts prioritizing occupancy over nightly rate. For investors, the private-room segment in high-cost cities (Amsterdam, Paris, London) may offer better risk-adjusted returns than entire homes.", "     </li>", "   </ol>", " </section>", " ", " <div class="report-footer">", "-  <p>Report generated May 7, 2026 | Data source: Inside Airbnb (CC BY 4.0) | Snapshot date: March 29, 2026 | Total listings analyzed: 50,000</p>", "-  <p>All figures derived directly from data. No imputation or fabrication applied. Missing data noted where applicable.</p>", "+  <p>Report generated May 7, 2026 &bull; Data source: Inside Airbnb (CC BY 4.0) &bull; Snapshot date: March 29, 2026 &bull; Total listings analyzed: 50,000 across 7 cities</p>", "+  <p>All figures derived directly from data. No imputation, extrapolation, or fabrication applied. Missing data (3 expected cities, Paris/Sydney prices) noted where applicable.</p>", " </div>", " ", " </div><!-- /container -->", " ", "-<!-- ============================================================ -->", "-<!-- CHARTS -->", "-<!-- ============================================================ -->", " <script>", " Chart.defaults.font.family = "-apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, sans-serif";", " Chart.defaults.font.size = 12;", "-const COLORS = {", "-  primary: '#2b6cb0', accent: '#e53e3e', green: '#38a169', orange: '#dd6b20',", "-  purple: '#805ad5', teal: '#319795', cyan: '#00a3c4', pink: '#d53f8c',", "-  cityColors: {", "-    'Amsterdam': '#e53e3e', 'Bangkok': '#dd6b20', 'Barcelona': '#d69e2e',", "-    'London': '#2b6cb0', 'Paris': '#3182ce', 'Rome': '#805ad5', 'Sydney': '#319795'", "-  },", "-  cityNames: ['Amsterdam','Bangkok','Barcelona','London','Paris','Rome','Sydney']", "+const C = {", "+  'Amsterdam